  "query_preview": "..\n(This afternoon through next Friday)\nIssued at 1125 AM CDT Sat Jul 25 2026\n\nA rather soggy and humid day is progressing across the Mid-South,\nwith a stationary boundary lingering with scattered showers and\nisolated thunderstorms. PWAT values remain between 2\" to 2.5\",\nwith most of the shower/thunderstorm coverage along and south of\nthe I-40 corridor. A few embedded thunderstorms have led to some\nperiods of heavy rainfall, so there will be a low chance for a\nlocalized flooding threat across southern TN into northeast MS\nthrough the afternoon hours as showers and thunderstorms continue\nto sag southeast. Given the cloud coverage, temperatures have \nstruggled so far today with much of the area remaining in the 70s \nto low 80s thus far. A few locations along the MS River and our \nfar south could approach heat index values of 105, but I do not \nthink this will be prolonged enough to need a Heat Advisory in \nthese locations. Any lingering showers and thunderstorms are \nexpected to move out overnight. With sky conditions slowly \nclearing, light winds, and generally low dew point depressions, we\nwill need to watch for some fog potential especially near the TN \nRiver overnight into tomorrow morning. Any fog that does develop \nwill quickly mix out following sunrise.\n\nInto tomorrow, oppressive heat and humidity will return to the\nMid-South with an upper-level ridge continuing to strengthen over\nthe Rockies into the Plains. With the high confidence in heat\nindices of at least 105 or greater for much of the area and\novernight temperatures remaining in the upper 70s, opted to run\nheat headlines from Sunday through at least Tuesday. The Extreme\nHeat Warning area will be generally the western two-thirds of the\narea, with a Heat Advisory encompassing locations along and just\nwest of the TN River and into northeast MS. An expansion of the\nwarning area could be needed Monday and / or Tuesday, but will\naddress the need closer to each day. Regardless, prepare for a\nreturn of some oppressive heat on Sunday through at least Tuesday\nwith high temperatures in the mid to upper 90s. Outside of the\noppressive heat, CAM guidance continues to show a decaying MCS\nthrough the Ohio River Valley late Sunday into early Monday. The\nlocation of this MCS has been mostly to our east, but a few CAMs\ndo want to bring some PoPs to our far eastern counties. Given the\nrun to run variability and overall little confidence, opted to\nleave negligible PoPs in for now from the NBM, but this will be\nsomething to watch into future forecast packages.\n\nBy Wednesday, a weak frontal boundary will move through the\nregion, allowing for temperatures and humidity to back off ever\nso slightly. While high temperatures will likely remain in the\nlow to mid 90s through the end of the week, dew point\ntemperatures will fall back to the upper 60s. Some locations\nacross the delta regions may need a Heat Advisory on Wednesday\nbefore the frontal boundary arrives, but uncertainty remains.\nWith a typical, summertime pattern finishing out next week, we\ncould see a pattern shift with increased PoPs into next weekend.",
